course: dictionary
lesson: 7863
title: Railroad, railway and the crossing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: In American English, a railroad is what British English calls a railway: the steel rails that trains run on, and the whole train system. «A boy in Ohio ducked under the lowered barrier at a railroad crossing» tells you a railroad crossing is a place where a road meets the tracks.
explain: Railroad can also be a verb, and this use is informal. To railroad someone means to push them into doing something quickly, without giving them time to think. Ask yourself: does the sentence show someone being rushed along, like a train on a track with no way to stop?
explain: Be careful with names. The Underground Railroad was not a train line. It was a secret network that helped enslaved people escape, and it used railway words such as stations and conductors as secret code.
fact: In American English, railroad means railway.
fact: A railroad crossing is where a road and a railroad track meet.
fact: «A train is heavy and fast and needs a very long way to stop», so you stop at a level crossing or railroad crossing when the lights flash, the bells ring or the barriers come down.
fact: As a verb, railroad is informal and means to force someone to act quickly without time to think.
fact: The Underground Railroad was a secret network that helped enslaved people escape; Harriet Tubman escaped and went back many times to lead others to freedom.
exercise: In American English, what does railroad mean? | A long bridge over a river | A train driver's uniform | *A railway, with steel rails that trains run on | A road for cars only
exercise: Someone says, «Don't let them railroad you into deciding today.» What does railroad mean here? | To travel slowly by train | *To push you into deciding quickly, without time to think | To lay new steel rails | To take a photo of a train
exercise: «At a level crossing or railroad crossing you stop when the barriers come down.» What should you do when the barriers come down? | Wave to the driver | Duck under them | *Stop | Speed up to get across first
test: Why does a train need so much room to stop? | Because it has no brakes | *Because it is heavy and fast, so it needs a very long way to stop | Because it is always a short train | Because it runs on grass
test: What were the secret railway words used by the Underground Railroad? | *Stations and conductors | Engines and carriages | Rails and sleepers | Tickets and platforms
exam: Which sentence uses railroad as a verb? | We waited at the railroad crossing for the train. | *The boss tried to railroad us into a decision before we had time to think. | Harriet Tubman helped people escape along the Underground Railroad. | The railroad runs from the east coast to the west.
